+++ job_c/satellite.help/satellite.variable.file/help_text.txt	2026-10-07 12:51:31.881598451 -0400
@@ -0,0 +1,177 @@
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+SATELLITE 004: satellite.variable.file
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+satellite.variable.file is a text file held as a list of its lines, counting
+from 1, that lives on the disk. The author: "we'll build it so you can iterate
+over the lines as if they were objects ... As if each line were a list of
+objects". So the words a satellite.container.list has are the words a file
+has, spelled the same and meaning the same thing done to lines: f.append(x)
+puts a line on the end, f[n] reads line n, f.size is how many lines there are.
+Learning the list teaches you the file.
+
+A handle comes from satellite.file.new(path), which makes a file that is not
+there, or satellite.file.open(path), which opens one that is -- see
+satellite.help(satellite.file) for the words, the kinds and where a path
+points. A handle is a reference, as a spacesuit's object is: g = f is a second
+name for ONE open file, not a copy, so a line appended through g is there
+through f, closing through either closes both, and f == g is true only when the
+two names hold the same file. A handle that would not open is a value, not an
+error: its ok is false, its error says why, and the program goes on.
+
+Line n is a satellite.variable.string, without the newline that ends it on the
+disk; that newline is the file's. f[n] reads line n, and n counts from 1: f[1]
+is the first line and f[f.size] the last. The list's words, for lines:
+
+    f.append(x)            x becomes a new last line
+    f.insert(n, x)         x becomes line n, and line n and every line below
+                           it move down one; n may be f.size + 1, the end
+    f.replace(n, x)        line n becomes x
+    f.replace(a, b)        the first a in the file, looking from line 1 down
+                           and inside one line, becomes b -- once, so a b
+                           that holds a is not replaced again
+    f.index_of(x)          the number of the first line that IS x, or 0
+    f.search(x)            the number of the first line that CONTAINS x, or 0;
+                           f[n].find(x) then says where in the line
+    f.contains(x)          whether any line is exactly x
+    f.remove_at(n)         line n out; the lines below move up one
+    f.remove(x)            the first line that is exactly x, out
+    f.remove_first()       line 1 out
+    f.remove_last()        the last line out
+    f.truncate(n)          keeps the first n lines
+    f.clear                no lines; the file stays
+    f.size                 how many lines
+    f.empty                whether there are none
+    f.first   f.last       line 1, and line f.size
+
+replace has two shapes under one name, and the kind of its first argument
+chooses: a number means a line, a string means text. 0 is never a line, so it
+is what index_of and search answer for "no such line", and a program tests it
+with > 0. A string holding a newline becomes that many lines when it is
+appended, inserted, or put in by replace. Where a word takes text and is given
+a number, the number's digits are taken and S020 goes to satellite.log.
+
+The disk's words:
+
+    f.save()               every change is on the disk now
+    f.read_all             the whole file as one string, newlines included,
+                           with every change not yet saved
+    f.close()              saves, then closes; false means the changes did
+                           not land, the handle stays open holding them, and
+                           error says why
+    f.open()               opens a closed handle again, reading the disk
+
+A save is whole or not at all: the new text is written beside the file and
+renamed over it, so a crash or a Ctrl-C leaves the old file or the new one,
+never a mixture. Appending is the fast path -- while nothing but appends has
+happened, the new lines stream to the end of the file as they pass 64 KiB,
+and nothing before them is rewritten. You need not close a file: every file a
+capsule or a block holds is saved and closed when that capsule or block ends,
+satellite.main's included, and at the prompt every open file is saved after
+every line and closed when the session ends. A save that cannot land at the
+end of a run is said, and the run exits 44 (S504) instead of 0.
+
+The handle's own words, which answer whether or not the file is open:
+
+    f.ok                   whether the file is open and usable
+    f.error                why the last thing failed, in words, or ""
+    f.path                 the whole path, from /, that the handle opened
+    f.exists               whether a file is still at that path
+
+And the author's lock, which every object carries, off: f.lock() turns it on,
+and from then every word used on the file through any of its names holds it,
+so threads sharing one file do not write over each other; f.unlock() turns it
+off again. A file nobody locks costs nothing for it.
+
+What stops a program, by design, is reading what is not there: f[n], f.first
+or f.last past the end (S501, exit 47), and any question about the lines of a
+file that is not open -- size, empty, first, last, index_of, search, contains,
+read_all -- because the only answer would be a made-up one (S503, exit 45). A
+line number below zero, given to any word, is S410 (exit 19), and f[0] is
+S413 (exit 66), since satellite counts from 1. What never stops a program is a
+word that changes the file: append, insert, replace, remove, truncate, clear,
+save and close answer true when they did it and false when they did not, and
+f.error says why -- line 0, a line past the end, text that is not in the file,
+a file that is closed. Nor do ok, error, path and exists, which answer for a
+handle in any state.
+
+A program may rewrite its own .satl while it runs. satl reads every file of a
+program once, before anything runs, and runs that copy, so the file on the disk
+is free: the author's build counter opens its own source, raises the number in
+it, and the copy that is running still says the old one. A report quotes the
+loaded copy, never the rewritten line. arguments.program is the program's own
+file, as it was given to satl.
+
+Not built yet, in today's words: a read-only open (satellite.file.open(path,
+"text", "read"), refused before anything runs today); binary files (the handle
+is not ok, and its error says so); and the author's .write, .position and
+.read, his file model of 2026-09-24, which no handle has today. Two handles on
+one path do not know of each other: each saves what it holds, and the later
+save is what the disk ends with.
+
+example:
+
+satellite.include(satellite)
+
+satellite.capsule satellite.main()
+{
+    satellite.variable.file my_file = satellite.file.new("demo.se", "text")
+    my_file.append("line_1")
+    my_file.append("line_2")
+    my_file.insert(2, "line_2")
+    my_file.replace(2, "line_3")
+    my_file.replace("line_2", "line_4")
+    satellite.statement.for(satellite.variable.number i = 1; i <= my_file.size; i++)
+    {
+        satellite.console.display(my_file[i]) // line_1, then line_3, then line_4
+    }
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.size) // 3
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.first + " " + my_file.last) // line_1 line_4
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.index_of("line_3")) // 2
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.search("4")) // 3
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.index_of("nowhere")) // 0
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.contains("line_1")) // true
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.remove_at(9)) // false
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.error) // line 9 is past the end of ...demo.se, which has 3 lines
+    satellite.variable.file same = my_file
+    same.append("line_5")
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.size) // 4
+    satellite.console.display(my_file == same) // true
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.close()) // true
+    satellite.console.display(my_file.ok) // false
+    satellite.variable.file again = satellite.file.open("demo.se")
+    satellite.console.display(again == my_file) // false
+    satellite.console.display(again.read_all) // line_1, line_3, line_4 and line_5, each on its own line
+    again.remove_first()
+    again.truncate(2)
+    satellite.console.display(again.size) // 2
+    satellite.console.display(again.last) // line_4
+    satellite.return(satellite)
+}
+
+a program that raises the build number in its own source, each time it runs:
+
+satellite.include(satellite)
+
+satellite.capsule satellite.main()
+{
+    satellite.variable.number build = 1
+    satellite.variable.file me = satellite.file.open(arguments.program)
+    satellite.variable.number at = me.search("satellite.variable.number build = ")
+    me.replace(at, "    satellite.variable.number build = " + (build + 1).string)
+    satellite.console.display("build " + build) // build 1 -- and the file now says 2, so the next run says build 2
+    satellite.return(satellite)
+}
+
+refused when it runs -- a line that is not there (S501, exit 47):
+
+satellite.include(satellite)
+
+satellite.capsule satellite.main()
+{
+    satellite.variable.file f = satellite.file.new("one.se")
+    f.append("the only line")
+    satellite.console.display(f[1]) // the only line
+    satellite.console.display(f[2]) // stops here: S501 LINE_PAST_THE_END, exit 47
+    satellite.return(satellite)
+}
